Linux operators are essential tools in shell scripting and command-line operations, enabling users to perform arithmetic, 
logical, relational, file testing, and redirection tasks. These operators streamline workflows and enhance script functionality.
Semicolon ; (command separator): it is used to run two or more command in a line and also these run in squence.if first command is incorrect it will give error and run other command.
Ampersand & : This operator allows you to run commands in the background of your terminal.
Dollar question-mark $?: show the status of last command run in terminal.
Logical And operator &&: The && operator in Linux is a logical AND operator used to combine multiple commands. It ensures that the subsequent command executes only if the preceding command is successful (returns a zero exit status)
Output Redirection > : In Linux, redirection operators are used to control the flow of data between commands and files.for example: ls > files.txt
>>: This operator does the same function of the > operator but appends the output rather than replacing (meaning nothing is overwritten)
Pipe | :Piping is a technique used in command-line environments that allows the output of one command to be used as the input for another. This creates a sequence of operations where the data flows from one command to the next. Represented by the | symbol, piping is widely used in the Windows CLI, as introduced earlier in this module, as well as in Unix-based shells.
